                <h2>Pre-Check: Confirm the Suspension Reason</h2>
<p>Before preparing appeal materials, log in to Google Ads and check your account status. Common suspension reasons include: policy violations, abnormal payment behavior, suspicious traffic patterns, or brand infringement complaints.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip</strong>: If a new account is suspended right after the first top-up, it's usually related to payment methods or address verification. For accounts with stable history suddenly suspended, it's likely due to traffic quality or policy violations.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Navigate to <strong>Settings → Account Preferences → Account Status</strong> to find the specific suspension code (Policy, Payment, Suspicious Activity). This determines the focus of your appeal materials.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Gather Basic Information</h2>
<p>Before filing an appeal, prepare the following:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Google Ads Customer ID</strong>: 10-12 digits, located in the upper right corner</li>
<li><strong>MCC Account ID (if applicable)</strong>: Used for managing multiple accounts</li>
<li><strong>Suspended ad campaigns or ads</strong>: Screenshots of violating content</li>
<li><strong>Target countries/regions and audiences</strong></li>
<li><strong>Website URL or App package name</strong>: Ensure the landing page is accessible</li>
</ol>
<p>These will be repeatedly used when filling out the appeal form. Organize them into a document for easy reference.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Draft the Appeal Statement</h2>
<p>This is the core of your appeal materials. Use concise, professional language to explain:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Understanding of the violation</strong>: Acknowledge the specific policy violated</li>
<li><strong>Corrective actions taken</strong>: Such as modifying ad copy, pausing violating campaigns, updating landing pages</li>
<li><strong>Preventive mechanisms</strong>: Such as new creative review processes, customer service training plans</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Writing Tips</strong>: Avoid overly technical jargon or expressing dissatisfaction. The tone should be sincere and professional, like writing an improvement report to a partner.</p>
<pre><code>Example Structure:
1. We understand the account was suspended due to [specific reason]
2. We have completed the following corrections on [date]:
   - Paused violating ads
   - Modified [specific content]
3. We have established [specific mechanism] to prevent recurrence
</code></pre>
<h2>Step 3: Prepare Supporting Documents</h2>
<p>Depending on the violation type, prepare corresponding documents:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Violation Type</th>
<th>Required Documents</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Policy violation (counterfeit, misrepresentation)</td>
<td>Business license, product authorization, brand registration certificate</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Payment issue</td>
<td>Credit card/corporate account authorization, payment records</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Suspicious traffic</td>
<td>Third-party monitoring report, traffic source explanation, GA screenshots</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Trademark complaint</td>
<td>Trademark registration certificate, court judgment (if any)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: All foreign documents require English translations or notarized copies. Websites in regulated industries (healthcare, finance) need corresponding licenses.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 4: Submit the Appeal</h2>
<ol>
<li>Go to Google Ads Help Center, click "Contact Us"</li>
<li>Select "Advertising Policies" or "Account Access" issue type</li>
<li>Fill out the appeal form, upload materials from Step 2 and Step 3</li>
<li>Record the ticket number (Case ID) after submission</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Within 24-48 hours</strong> after submission, Google's team usually sends an initial response. If no response after 5 business days, follow up through the same ticket.</p>
<h2>Step 5: Follow Up and Review</h2>
<h3>Handling Appeal Results</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Appeal approved</strong>: Immediately verify if the account resumes normal operation; confirm ad status before launching new budgets</li>
<li><strong>Appeal rejected</strong>: Read the rejection reason carefully, supplement materials and resubmit, or escalate to Google's advanced support team</li>
<li><strong>No response for extended time</strong>: Check progress through Google Ads official Twitter (@GoogleAds) or partner support channels</li>
</ul>
<h3>Internal Review Mechanism</h3>
<p>Regardless of the outcome, conduct an internal review:</p>
<ol>
<li>Which trigger caused the violation—creative, landing page, or targeting?</li>
<li>Can the current review process detect similar issues in advance?</li>
<li>Is a human + automated dual-review mechanism needed?</li>
</ol>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: What happens to the balance after suspension?</strong><br />
A: The balance is usually refunded to the original payment method after the appeal is resolved, or applied to future campaigns. Verify the refund before deciding whether to reopen.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Can someone else file the appeal on my behalf?</strong><br />
A: Google recommends the account holder submit directly. If using a third party, written authorization is required, otherwise the appeal may be rejected.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Can I continue after 3 rejections?</strong><br />
A: Yes, but pause campaigns and make thorough corrections first. Repeatedly submitting the same content will be flagged by the system, lowering approval rates.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Quick Checklist</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Confirmed suspension reason and specific violation code</li>
<li>[ ] Organized customer ID, campaign screenshots, landing page URLs</li>
<li>[ ] Completed appeal statement with corrective and preventive actions</li>
<li>[ ] Prepared business license, authorization documents</li>
<li>[ ] Submitted through official channels and recorded ticket number</li>
<li>[ ] Established internal review mechanism to prevent recurrence</li>
</ul>
